course: books
lesson: 58
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IX, part 3: Wayfarers All (the Sea Rat from Constantinople)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: As the Rat lies by the lane, a tired, dusty traveller comes along: another rat, lean and sharp-faced, with gold ear-rings, a faded blue jersey and his things tied up in a blue handkerchief. He sits down beside the Water Rat, and the two rest in friendly silence.
explain: Sniffing the air, the stranger names the clover, the cows, the reapers and the moorhen, and says the river life is «the best in the world, if only you are strong enough to lead it» — but he has just left six months of farm life and is tramping south, back to the sea.
explain: He is a seafaring rat from Constantinople; his ancestor came there from Norway with King Sigurd's sixty ships, and any harbour feels like home to him.
explain: He doesn't sail great oceans: he is «in the coasting trade», always near land, and loves the jolly times on shore. The Water Rat, who feels his own life is «narrow» today, begs to hear about his voyages.
explain: So the Sea Rat tells of his last trip: family troubles, then the Greek islands, warm nights under the stars, up the Adriatic and into Venice «down a path of gold», with music on the Grand Canal and gondolas packed side by side.
fact: The traveller greets the Rat «with a gesture of courtesy that had something foreign about it»; the Rat lets him rest «unquestioned», knowing «the value all animals attach at times to mere silent companionship».
fact: He is «lean and keen-featured» (sharp-faced), «somewhat bowed at the shoulders», with «small gold ear rings», «a knitted jersey ... of a faded blue», patched breeches, and his belongings «tied up in a blue cotton handkerchief».
fact: He reads the countryside with his nose and ears: «That was clover»; cows «cropping the grass»; «distant reapers»; cottage smoke; and «the call of a moorhen» tells him the river is near.
fact: «I see by your build that you're a freshwater mariner»: a sailor of rivers, not of the salt sea.
fact: «It is a goodly life that you lead, friend; no doubt the best in the world, if only you are strong enough to lead it!» The Water Rat answers «Yes, it's the life, the only life, to live», but «without his usual whole-hearted conviction».
fact: The stranger has tried the quiet life, «six months of it», on a «Nice little farm ... Upalong in that direction» (upalong = up that way, in country talk), and now is «tramping southward, following the old call, back to the old life».
fact: «I'm a seafaring rat, I am, and the port I originally hail from is Constantinople» (hail from = come from). Constantinople is the old name of Istanbul in Turkey.
fact: He tells how «Sigurd, King of Norway» sailed there «with sixty ships», through streets hung «with purple and gold», and dined with the Emperor and Empress; many Northmen stayed in the Emperor's body-guard, and «my ancestor, a Norwegian born, stayed behind too». King Sigurd's visit really happened, about nine hundred years ago.
fact: Any port is home to him: «Set me down on any of their quays or foreshores, and I am home again» (a quay is a stone landing-place for ships).
fact: He is not a deep-ocean sailor: «I'm in the coasting trade, and rarely out of sight of land. It's the jolly times on shore that appeal to me».
fact: The Water Rat confesses: «my life, I confess to you, feels to me to-day somewhat narrow and circumscribed» (circumscribed = closed in, limited).
fact: His last voyage began with «Family troubles, as usual. The domestic storm-cone was hoisted», so he shipped on a small trading vessel bound from Constantinople — a storm-cone is a signal hung up in harbours to warn of a storm, so this is a joke meaning there were quarrels at home.
fact: He sailed to «the Grecian Islands and the Levant» (the lands at the eastern end of the Mediterranean Sea), sleeping by day «in some cool temple or ruined cistern» (an old stone water-tank), feasting «under great stars set in a velvet sky».
fact: Then «up the Adriatic» (the sea between Italy and the Balkans) and «into Venice down a path of gold», as the sun rose behind them.
fact: In Venice, by the Grand Canal at night, «the air is full of music and the sky full of stars», and the gondolas (long thin Venice boats) are «packed so that you could walk across the canal on them from side to side!» — «do you like shellfish? Well, well, we won't linger over that now.»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «lekker» (South Africa) = nice; «craic» (Ireland) = fun, good times; «gadgie» (North East England) = bloke; «cap» = a lie; «stan» = be a big fan of.
exercise: Who comes walking along the lane? | A tired, dusty rat | Toad, still in his bonnet | The Mole, back from the river | A swallow who has hurt her wing
exercise: How does the stranger greet the Water Rat? | With a polite gesture that seems foreign | With a loud shout of «Hullo, Ratty!» across the field | With a deep bow and a long speech in French | With a growl, as if the Rat were in his way
exercise: What does the Water Rat do while the stranger rests? | Lets him rest without asking questions | Asks him a hundred questions at once about his travels | Runs home to fetch the Mole and Badger to meet him | Goes on reading his book of poems aloud to him
exercise: Which is part of the stranger's looks? | Small gold ear-rings | A red velvet smoking-suit | A tall shiny black hat | A pair of motoring goggles
exercise: What is he carrying his things in? | A blue cotton handkerchief | A leather suitcase with straps | A wicker luncheon-basket | A canvas sailor's bag on a pole
exercise: How does the stranger know the river is close? | He hears a moorhen calling | He can see the boat-house from the lane | He smells the mud of the bank | The Rat tells him so
exercise: What is a «freshwater mariner»? | A sailor of rivers, not the sea | A fish that lives in clean water | A boat painted fresh and new | A rat who never washes
exercise: What does the stranger say about the Water Rat's life? | It's the best, if you are strong enough to lead it | It's dull and nobody with any sense should stay in it | It's wonderful, and he will stay there for ever now | It's dangerous because of the weasels in the Wood
exercise: How does the Water Rat answer? | «It's the only life» — but not wholeheartedly | «It's the worst life of all, and I hate it» | «I don't know; I've never thought about it» | «Ask the Mole about that; he knows far more than I do about lives»
exercise: How long did the stranger try farm life? | Six months | Six years | Six weeks | Six days and nights
exercise: Where is the stranger going now? | South, back to the old life at sea | North, back to his nice little farm | To Toad Hall, to sell him a boat | To the castle, to visit a friend
exercise: What does «Upalong» mean? | Up that way | Up a tree | Upside down | Up all night
exercise: Where does the Sea Rat first come from? | Constantinople | Venice | The London River | A fjord in Norway
exercise: What is Constantinople called today? | Istanbul | Athens | Rome | Alexandria
exercise: Who was Sigurd? | A King of Norway who sailed there with sixty ships | An emperor who built a great palace by the sea | A pirate who stole the Emperor's purple and gold | The Sea Rat's own father, a famous captain of a great sailing ship
exercise: How did the Sea Rat's family come to Constantinople? | His Norwegian ancestor stayed behind there with Sigurd's ships | His grandmother was carried off by a storm | His father bought a shop there selling cheese | They were sent there long ago by the King of England as his messengers
exercise: Where does the Sea Rat feel at home? | In any port where ships come in | Only in his nice farm upalong | Only in Constantinople | Only far out on the deep ocean, away from all land
exercise: What is «the coasting trade»? | Sailing along coasts, rarely out of sight of land | Sailing across the widest oceans for months | Selling coats to sailors in every harbour | Rowing races along the river for silver cups and prizes
exercise: What does the Water Rat confess about his own life today? | It feels narrow and closed in | It feels far too busy and noisy | It is the best life anyone could have | It is too full of travel and change
exercise: What started the Sea Rat's last voyage? | Family troubles, as usual | A letter from King Sigurd | A shipwreck off Venice | A dream about shellfish
exercise: What is the joke in «The domestic storm-cone was hoisted»? | A harbour storm signal stands for quarrels at home | His house was really blown away by a great wind | He hung a cone-shaped hat on his front door | His family sold ice-cream cones to the sailors in the harbour all summer
exercise: Where did he sleep in the heat of the day in Greece? | In a cool temple or old water-tank | On the deck in the full sun | In a smart hotel by the sea | Up the tall mast in a little basket, rocking
exercise: How did they come into Venice? | Down a path of gold, as the sun rose behind them | On foot over the mountains, in deep snow at night | By train, through a long tunnel under the city | In a balloon, drifting over the rooftops at dawn
exercise: What is special about the gondolas at night? | So many that you could walk across the canal on them | They fly little flags with the Sea Rat's name | They are all painted gold, yellow and green | They race each other up and down the canal all night for silver prizes
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I, how did the Rat finish «It's my world, and…» about the River? | «…I don't want any other» | «…one day I'll leave it for the sea» | «…Badger's too» | «…it's far too small»
exercise: Looking back: in chapter II, which animal said «The whole world before you, and a horizon that's always changing!»? | Toad, about his caravan | The Rat, about his boat | Badger, about his tunnels | The Otter, about the weir
exercise: Looking back: in chapter IV, who told of a great city of people who came, built, and went away? | Badger | The Rat | The Otter | The hedgehogs
exercise: ★★★ The Water Rat says the stranger is «not one of us ... nor yet a farmer; nor even ... of this country». How many things does he say the stranger is NOT? | Three | Two | One | Four or more
exercise: ★★★ Careful: «I did not say exactly that». What had the Sea Rat said, and what did the Water Rat turn it into? | «the best ... if strong enough» became «the only life» | «the only life» became «the best life in the world» | «a dull life» became «a good life» | «the worst» became «the best»
test: Kerry from Galway: «the Sea Rat's farm was great craic, so he's staying for good.» (craic = fun) Put it right. | He left it after six months and is tramping south to the sea | Yes, he loves farming and will never go back to the sea | He never had a farm; he walked straight from a ship | He is going back north to his nice little farm tomorrow, after a rest
test: «The gadgie's from Norway, like, born there.» (gadgie = bloke) Put it right. | His ancestor was Norwegian; he hails from Constantinople | Yes, he was born in a fishing village in Norway | He was born in Venice on a gondola | He was born on the river bank near Toad Hall, like the Water Rat
test: Which is TRUE about the Sea Rat? | He likes the jolly times on shore as much as sailing | He spends months out of sight of land | He is the King of Norway's grandson | He has never been to Venice or any city by the sea
test: Why does the Water Rat answer «without his usual whole-hearted conviction»? | The swallows have already stirred a longing in him | He is ill with a cold and cannot think | He dislikes the stranger and wants him to go | He has never liked the river very much, as everyone knows
test: What does «if only you are strong enough to lead it» hint about the Sea Rat himself? | He was not «strong enough» to stay settled; the call took him | He is weak from hunger and needs a doctor | He thinks the Water Rat is too small for river life | He means that rowing a boat needs very strong arms and legs
test: Thabo from Durban: «lekker, the Sea Rat sails right across oceans for months.» (lekker = nice) Put it right. | He's in the coasting trade, rarely out of sight of land | Yes, he loves months with no land in sight | He has never sailed; he only walks | He sails only on the river, up and down with the Water Rat
test: «Sigurd's story is total cap.» (cap = a lie) Is it? | No: a Norwegian king really did sail to Constantinople long ago | Yes: Norway never had any kings at all | Yes: there was never a city called Constantinople | No: Sigurd is the name of the Water Rat's little blue-and-white boat
test: «I stan this guy, he's the best sailor ever.» (stan = be a big fan of) What does the Sea Rat himself say about the hard sailor's life? | Months out of sight of land «would not suit me at all» | He loves storms more than anything | He has sailed round the whole world twice | He says sailing is easy for everyone, even small river animals
test: What would the Water Rat probably have said to this stranger at the start of the book? | That the Wide World doesn't matter and he never wants to go | That he will pack his bags and come along at once | That he has been to Venice many times | That he hates the river and has always wanted to be a sailor on the seas
test: Looking back: what did the Mole do in chapter I that the Sea Rat has done too? | Left his old life suddenly when a call came | Stole a motor-car | Lived underground in a city | Sang carols at Christmas with the field-mice in the snow
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, which traveller met a fox at night who mocked his washing? | Toad, dressed as a washerwoman | The Sea Rat on his way south | The Mole, coming home from Mole End | The Rat, looking for Portly
test: Looking back: in chapter V, the Rat and the Mole peeped through village windows at families by the fire. What did they feel? | Wistful, being far from home themselves | Angry that they were not invited | Bored, and wanted to go on | Frightened of the cats and dogs inside by the fire
test: Looking back: in chapter I, which riverside creatures did the Rat complain were «always wanting you to do something»? | Otters, kingfishers, dabchicks and moorhens | Swallows, cuckoos and robins | Weasels, stoats and foxes | Toads, frogs and newts in the ditches
test: Looking back: in chapter VII, the Rat felt a call in music he could not resist. What calls the Sea Rat? | «the old call», back to the sea and the South | A letter from the Emperor | The bells of Venice | A song from the reeds by the water
test: ★★★ Put the voyage in order: Venice, Constantinople, Grecian Islands, Adriatic. | Constantinople, Grecian Islands, Adriatic, Venice | Venice, Adriatic, Grecian Islands, Constantinople | Grecian Islands, Constantinople, Venice, Adriatic | Adriatic, Venice, Constantinople, Grecian Islands
test: ★★★ «do you like shellfish? Well, well, we won't linger over that now.» Why does he stop himself? | Talking of food makes him hungrier; he will hint at it again later | He hates shellfish and feels sick | The Water Rat has told him to be quiet | He has quite forgotten what shellfish are and how they taste
exam: Identify the port from which the seafaring rat declared himself originally to hail. | Constantinople | Venice | Marseilles | The London River
exam: State the historical personage whose voyage is said to account for the presence of the seafarer's ancestor in that port. | Sigurd, King of Norway, who sailed thither with sixty ships | An Emperor of Constantinople, who sailed northward with a fleet of galleys | A merchant of Venice, who traded in spices with the Levant | The captain of a coasting vessel engaged in the wine trade
exam: Ascertain the nature of the seafarer's trade as described by himself. | The coasting trade, seldom out of sight of land | Deep-sea voyaging, for months together beyond sight of land | The command of a vessel of the Imperial navy | The keeping of an inn upon the quays of Venice
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the seafarer's sojourn upon the farm? | It endured some six months, at the conclusion of which he resumed his journey southward | It was intended to be permanent and remains so | It was spent in the company of the Water Rat and the Mole | It was terminated by his arrest for trespass upon the property of a neighbouring landowner
